l/honor-heavy waits for others.  \n- But: W4 is part of middle sequence; not dangerous to keep? Not particularly valuable either.\n\nOption 2: Play B1  \n- Only two B1s remain unseen; none have been punged. Previous player recently discarded B1 twice \u2014 possibly getting rid of extras, meaning they might be close to winning.  \n- If they are collecting lower bamboos or building chows like [B1,B2,B3], discarding B1 could feed them.  \n- However, B2 and B3 are low in count (only 1 each left), so [B1,B2,B3] is unlikely to be completed by others.  \n- I hold only one B1, and have no chow involving it. It's a loose tile.\n\nBut crucially: **My hand is already complete with four melds and a pair? Wait \u2014 do I have a pair?**  \nCurrent hand: [W4, B1] \u2192 no pair.  \nMelds: 4 sets \u2192 need a pair to win.  \nBut I don\u2019t have a pair yet. So I cannot win now. I must discard one, leaving the other as potential pair base.\n\nSo this is a decision: which tile to keep as potential pair base?\n\n- W4: Already part of a chow I used. Another W4 could form a pair. One W4 remains unseen (unshown = 3 total, I have 1, at least one used in my chow? No \u2014 wait: my chow [W2,W3,W4] uses one W4. I now hold another W4 \u2192 that makes two W4s used by me. Total of 4 exist. Two remain unshown. So two left in game. Possible someone else holds one.\n\nBut: the across player had [W3, W3, W3], and next has [W7,W8,W9], and I have [W2,W3,W4] \u2014 so W3 is gone. W4 still has availability.\n\nSimilarly, B1: 2 left. Also possible.\n\nBut here\u2019s key: **Can I win on a future draw?**\n\nI currently have:\n- Three chows (T5-7, B5-7, W2-4)\n- One pung (J3)\n- And two single tiles: W4, B1\n\nWait \u2014 this suggests I actually have **five** sets? No: the melds list says four: [[T5,T6,T7],[B5,B6,B7],[W2,W3,W4],[J3,J3,J3]] \u2192 that\u2019s four complete melds. Then hand is [W4, B1] \u2192 that means I have five tiles outside melds? Impossible.\n\nAh \u2014 critical realization: **The tile W4 appears both in a meld and in hand? That can't happen unless it's a promoted kong.**\n\nBut I did not promote [W2,W3,W4] into a kong \u2014 I only have a chow.\n\nTherefore, likely explanation: the system representation lists the *current* state. My meld [W2,W3,W4] consumed one W4. The W4 in hand is a different instance. So I have two W4s: one in meld, one in hand.\n\nTotal W4: 4 copies. Unshown: 2 \u2192 so two are already out (either discarded or used). I use one in meld, hold one \u2192 two accounted. So two others are somewhere \u2014 possibly one discarded, one held.\n\nSame for B1: I hold one, two unshown \u2192 two others exist.\n\nBut again: I have four melds already \u2192 that\u2019s 3\u00d74 = 12 tiles. Hand has 2 tiles \u2192 total 14 tiles. But a legal hand before draw is 13 tiles. So this must be **after drawing**, and now I must discard one to go to 13.\n\nYes \u2014 standard flow: draw \u2192 then discard.\n\nSo: I just drew a tile (either W4 or B1), bringing my hand to 14 tiles (including the two singles), and now I must discard one.\n\nBut I don\u2019t have a pair. So I cannot win unless one of these two tiles forms a pair? But I only have one of each.\n\nUnless... did I misread?\n\nHand: [W4: 1; B1: 1] \u2192 one W4, one B1.\n\nMelds: four sets \u2192 12 tiles.\n\nTotal: 14 tiles \u2192 yes, after draw.\n\nBut no pair \u2192 so I cannot declare win.\n\nThus, I must discard one, keeping the other as singleton, hoping to draw its duplicate later.\n\nSo the question is: which tile gives better chance to complete pair and potentially complete a scoring hand?\n\nNow evaluate potential patterns:\n\n- I have three chows and one pung (J3).\n- J3 pung is worth Dragon Pung (2 points).\n- I have tiles across suits: T, B, W, J \u2192 All Types? Yes: three suits, dragons, winds? I have F3 pung from across, but do I have wind meld? No. My melds: no wind. But I may have held wind? No. So no All Types.\n- Half Flush? No \u2014 three suits present.\n- All Chows? No \u2014 I have a pung.\n- All Pungs? No \u2014 I have three chows.\n- Outside Hand? Only if every meld has terminals or honors. But T5-7, B5-7, W2-4 are internal sequences \u2014 not containing 1 or 9. So not Outside Hand.\n- My J3 pung is good \u2014 2 points.\n- Seat Wind is East (F1), Prevailing Wind is West (F3). I have no F1 or F3 in my melds. Across player has F3 pung, so I can't use that.\n- So no Prevalent Wind or Seat Wind bonus.\n\nBut perhaps going for **Seven Pairs**? No \u2014 too far.\n\nOr **All Simples**? But W2-4 includes W2 and W4 \u2014 not terminals. T5-7: internal. B5-7: same. But W2-4: W2 is not terminal. Terminal is 1 or 9. So all are simples? Yes \u2014 no 1 or 9 in any chow. J3 is honor \u2014 breaks All Simples.\n\nSo not All Simples.\n\nWait: **J3 is White Dragon \u2014 honor**, so any hand with it has honor tile.\n\nSo only possible patterns are those allowing honors.\n\nNow: I have three chows in different suits: T, B, W \u2014 all number 5-7 range.\n\nIs there **Mixed Triple Chow**? That requires three chows with same numbers in different suits. But mine are T5-7, B5-7, W2-3-4 \u2192 different numbers. W chow is 2-3-4, others 5-6-7 \u2192 not matching.\n\nNo Pure Triple Chow.\n\nNo Pure Straight \u2014 that needs 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9 in same suit.\n\nMy best hope is **All Pungs** (6 points) + Dragon Pung (2) + Concealed Kong? No \u2014 all melds exposed.\n\nBut I have only one pung so far.\n\nAlternatively, **Half Flush**? No \u2014 three suits.\n\nPerhaps **Mixed Shifted Chows**: three consecutive chows shifted by one: e.g., 2-3-4, 3-4-5, 4-5-6 \u2014 but I have T5-7, B5-7, W2-4 \u2014 not consecutive.\n\nNo clear high-scoring pattern.\n\nBut look: I have J3 pung \u2014 2 points.\n\nIf I can get a pair, maybe aim for minimal 8-point hand.\n\nPossible low-point patterns:\n- All Chows: 2 points \u2014 but I have a pung.\n- Melded Hand: 6 points \u2014 requires all melds formed by stealing, and winning tile from opponent. My melds: [T5,T6,T7] via Chi T6 \u2192 stolen. [B5,B6,B7] via Chi B6 \u2192 stolen. [W2,W3,W4] via Chi W3 \u2192 stolen. [J3,J3,J3] via Peng J3 \u2192 stolen. So all melds are exposed and formed by stealing. That qualifies for **Melded Hand** (6 points).\n- Plus, Dragon Pung (J3) = 2 points.\n- Total: 8 points \u2014 minimum to win.\n\nSo if I can complete a legal hand (four melds and a pair), with all melds stolen, and I win by stealing the final tile (pair), then I get Melded Hand (6) + Dragon Pung (2) = 8.\n\nPerfect.\n\nBut currently, I have no pair. I must eventually win by claiming a tile to complete the pair.\n\nImportant: **Melded Hand** requires that the pair is completed by a tile from another player \u2014 i.e., I must win by stealing a discard to make the pair.\n\nTherefore, I must **not** draw the winning tile \u2014 I must claim it.\n\nMoreover, I must keep a pairless hand until then.\n\nNow, back to current move: I must discard either W4 or B1.\n\nWhich discard is safer and preserves more winning potential?\n\nConsider danger:\n- If I discard B1: previous player recently discarded B1 twice. That often means they are getting rid of duplicates \u2014 possibly they already have a pair or are avoiding terminal pairs. Or they are discarding safely because they don\u2019t need it.\n- But if they are waiting for B1 to complete a pair, discarding it lets them win.\n- Similarly, W4: I used it in a chow, across player has W3s and W6s, next has W7-9 \u2014 so W4 is less likely to complete their hand.\n\nAlso, unshown counts:\n- B1: 2 left \u2192 higher availability.\n- W4: 2 left \u2192 same.\n\nBut B1 is a terminal (1), W4 is simple.\n\nA player needing a terminal pair might wait on B1.\n\nBut more importantly: **which tile gives me better chance to eventually win by steal?**\n\nI need to keep one of W4 or B1, and hope the other player discards its duplicate.\n\nBut if I discard W4, I keep B1 \u2192 then I am waiting for someone to discard B1 to make my pair.\n\nSame for W4.\n\nBut: **I already have a W4 in a meld** \u2014 so opponents know I am interested in W4. I used it in a chow. Whereas B1 \u2014 I have never shown interest. So B1 is more concealed.\n\nTherefore, keeping B1 is less telegraphed.\n\nAlso, previous player discarded B1 twice \u2014 they may be out of B1s, so less likely to discard a third? But players only have four copies. They played B1 twice \u2014 maybe they had two, got rid. So B1 might be relatively safe.\n\nBut if I discard W4, I keep B1 \u2014 and B1 is less expected.\n\nMoreover, W4 is in the middle \u2014 more commonly used in chows. Someone may need W4 for [W3,W4,W5] or [W4,W5,W6]. But W3 is exhausted (across player has pung, and I used one), so [W3,W4,W5] impossible. W5: only one left. So W4 is less useful.\n\nSimilarly, B1: could be used in [B1,B2,B3] \u2014 but B2 and B3 only one left each. So very hard to complete.\n\nSo both are relatively safe to discard.\n\nBut since I must choose one to keep for potential pair, and I want to maximize chance of someone discarding the one I need, I should keep the one that is **less obvious**.\n\nB1 is better for that.\n\nAlso, consider scoring: if I win with pair of B1 (terminal), does that affect patterns? B1 is terminal, so if I have a terminal pair, it doesn't break anything. But for Melded Hand, it doesn't matter.\n\nAnother point: **Last Tile Claim** or **Robbing Kong** could give extra points, but not relevant now.\n\nFinal consideration: **Unshown tiles** \u2014 B1 has 2 left, W4 has 2 left \u2014 symmetric.\n\nBut: I already used W4 in a chow \u2014 so I've shown two W4s (one in chow, one in hand about to discard). So if I discard W4, I reveal that I had two, suggesting possible pair attempt. But I didn't win, so they know I didn't have pair.\n\nBut B1 \u2014 I\u2019ve never touched it. More stealthy.\n\nTherefore, better to **keep B1**, and **discard W4**.\n\nBecause:\n- W4 is more exposed \u2014 I used it in a chow.\n- B1 is cleaner, less visible, better candidate for surprise win via steal.\n- Both are similarly rare, but B1 as terminal might be slightly riskier to keep, but since I'm not drawing yet, it's fine.\n\nWait \u2014 I must discard one. I want to keep the better candidate for future pair.\n\nSo keep B1, discard W4.\n\nAnswer: Play W4\n\nReason: To retain B1 as a concealed pair base for a potential win via discard steal, supporting the Melded Hand (6 points) + Dragon Pung (2 points) = 8-point qualifying hand. Discarding W4 is safe as it's partially exposed through prior chow and less critical for opponents due to lack of adjacent tiles (W3 exhausted).\n\nSituation: I hold W4 and B1 after drawing, with four exposed melds formed via Chi/Peng. I lack a pair to win. Valid actions are to discard W4 or B1. I aim for Melded Hand (6 pts) + Dragon Pung (2 pts) = 8 pts.\nEvaluation: Discarding W4 allows keeping B1, which is less exposed than W4 (used in chow). B1 is a better stealth candidate for a future pair. Both tiles are relatively safe to discard due to limited use in current board state.\nReason: Keeping B1 maximizes chances for a concealed win via discard steal, fulfilling Melded Hand requirements. W4 is more telegraphed due to prior use in a chow.\nAnswer: Play W4"}
